# Public site i18n (EN → ES / ZH / VI / TL)

Jay will describe website changes against the **English** public pages. Apply those same changes to the **Spanish**, **Chinese**, **Vietnamese**, and **Tagalog** versions unless he explicitly says English-only.

## Scope

- **Mirror:** `/impact`, `/about`, `/privacy`, `/contact` (and their `/es/…`, `/zh/…`, `/vi/…`, `/tl/…` URLs)
- **Do not localize:** `/dev`, developer login, or other developer-only UI

## How copy is stored

- Shared markup lives in `public/impact/`, `public/privacy/`, `public/contact/`
- User-facing strings live in `public/i18n/messages.js` under `en`, `es`, `zh`, `vi`, and `tl`
- Language switching is handled by `public/i18n/lang.js` (`data-i18n*` attributes)

## When changing the English page

1. Update the English HTML/CSS/JS structure as requested.
2. Add or update the matching keys in `messages.js` for **en**, **es**, **zh**, **vi**, and **tl**.
3. Keep `data-i18n` / `data-i18n-html` / `data-i18n-aria` / `data-i18n-alt` keys in sync with the markup.
4. Translate ES/ZH/VI/TL to match the English meaning; do not leave new English-only UI on `/es`, `/zh`, `/vi`, or `/tl`.
5. If a structural change has no copy (layout, CSS, assets), it automatically applies to all languages – still verify nothing language-specific broke.

## Examples

- New banner line → update `impact.bannerLine` (or relevant key) in `en`, `es`, `zh`, `vi`, and `tl`.
- New section on Impact → same DOM for all langs + new message keys in all five locales.
- “Change the CTA to X” → English CTA string plus Spanish, Chinese, Vietnamese, and Tagalog equivalents.
